{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness of the lotteries, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes. In this case, both lotteries involve negative outcomes (losses), so we assess the distribution of losses. Lottery A has a 45% chance of a -6000€ loss and a 55% chance of 0€, meaning 55% of the probability is on a neutral (no loss) outcome. Lottery B has a 90% chance of a -3000€ loss and a 10% chance of 0€, meaning 90% of the probability is on a loss. Thus, Lottery A has a larger portion of probability on neutral outcomes (55% vs. 10%), meaning it has less probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, Lottery A is less right-skewed (i.e., more left-skewed or balanced), while Lottery B is more right-skewed because it has a higher probability (90%) assigned to a loss (unfavorable outcome). According to the decision reason, the more right-skewed lottery is preferred. So, the reason would prefer Lottery B over Lottery A.",
